      "name": null,
      "problem": "The electrical resistivity of a copper wire varies with the temperature. The function $R(T)$ gives the resistivity of a wire at a temperature of $T$ degrees Celsius. What is the best interpretation of ${R}^{′}(10)=-0.01?$",
      "worked_solution": "The function ${R}^{′}(T)$ represents the rate at which the resistivity of the wire is decreasing at $T$ degrees Celsius.\n\nTherefore, since it is negative, ${R}^{′}(10)=-0.01$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *At $10$ degrees Celsius, the resistivity of the wire decreases at a rate of $0.01$ units per degree.*",
